+\subsection{Compact-to-slender carrier-state hypothesis}
+\label{subsec:rt_compact_to_slender_state}
+
+\textbf{[RESEARCH / STATE-DEPENDENT GEOMETRY].}
+The compact ideal trefoil and a long thin vortex filament are treated as
+different geometric regimes of one topological carrier, not as two
+simultaneous approximations to the same state.  Let
+\begin{equation}
+    \lambda
+    :=
+    \frac{L(\lambda)}{L_0}
+    \ge1,
+    \qquad
+    \mathbf X(1)\simeq\mathbf X_{\rm ideal,3_1},
+    \label{eq:rt_stretch_parameter}
+\end{equation}
+where $L_0$ is the compact reference length.  In the absence of reconnection,
+\begin{equation}
+    K[\mathbf X(\lambda)]=3_1
+    \label{eq:rt_stretch_topology_guard}
+\end{equation}
+throughout the deformation.  Equation~\eqref{eq:rt_stretch_parameter} is a
+state-coordinate ansatz, not an assertion that a physical trajectory with all
+values of $\lambda$ has already been constructed.
+
+\paragraph{Conditional material-volume scaling.}
+If the core is a material tube of conserved volume and effective cross-section
+$A_{\rm core}(\lambda)$, incompressibility gives
+\begin{equation}
+    A_{\rm core}(\lambda)L(\lambda)
+    =
+    A_0L_0.
+    \label{eq:rt_core_volume_conservation}
+\end{equation}
+For an approximately circular cross-section,
+$A_{\rm core}=\pi a^2$, this becomes
+\begin{equation}
+    a(\lambda)
+    =
+    \frac{a_0}{\sqrt{\lambda}}.
+    \label{eq:rt_core_radius_stretch_scaling}
+\end{equation}
+This scaling is conditional.  It must not be used if the chosen core boundary
+is not material, if fluid crosses it, if the vorticity profile changes its
+support definition, or if $a$ is only a fitted effective regularization length.
+
+The transition to a slender state is accepted only when the measured
+quantities
+\begin{equation}
+    \epsilon_{\kappa}(\lambda)
+    =
+    a(\lambda)\kappa_{\max}(\lambda),
+    \qquad
+    \epsilon_{\rm sep}(\lambda)
+    =
+    \frac{a(\lambda)}{d_{\rm sep}(\lambda)}
+    \label{eq:rt_stretched_state_slenderness}
+\end{equation}
+are both small and the nonlocality ratio in
+Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_kam_nonlocality_ratio} is perturbative.  Increasing $L$ alone
+does not prove slenderness because curvature and nonlocal separation can change
+at the same time.
+
+\paragraph{Compton-scale excitation hypothesis.}
+Let $E_{\rm tr}$ denote the energy actually transferred to the internal carrier
+by a kinematically allowed interaction, and define
+\begin{equation}
+    \eta_{\rm tr}
+    :=
+    \frac{E_{\rm tr}}{m_ec^2}.
+    \label{eq:rt_compton_transfer_parameter}
+\end{equation}
+The conjecture that strong stretching begins for
+$\eta_{\rm tr}=O(1)$ is \textbf{[HYPOTHESIS]}.  The required transition condition
+is
+\begin{equation}
+    E_{\rm tr}
+    \ge
+    \Delta E_{\rm carrier}(\lambda)
+    :=
+    E_{\rm carrier}[\mathbf X(\lambda),a(\lambda)]
+    -
+    E_{\rm carrier}[\mathbf X(1),a_0],
+    \label{eq:rt_stretch_energy_gate}
+\end{equation}
+with momentum and angular-momentum conservation imposed in the same process.
+For photon-mediated excitation, $E_{\rm tr}$ is not automatically equal to
+$h\nu$: a free electron in vacuum cannot absorb one isolated photon while
+remaining an on-shell electron.  The model must specify Compton scattering, a
+bound state, an external field, a third body, or another allowed transfer
+channel.  Therefore
+\begin{equation}
+    h\nu\gtrsim m_ec^2
+    \quad\not\Rightarrow\quad
+    \lambda\gg1
+    \label{eq:rt_compton_not_automatic_stretch}
+\end{equation}
+without an explicit interaction and a computed
+$\Delta E_{\rm carrier}(\lambda)$.
+
+\paragraph{Planck-scale uniform-core obstruction.}
+Consider only the conditional combination
+\begin{equation}
+    a_0=\rc,
+    \qquad
+    L_0=\operatorname{Rop}_{\rm rad}(3_1)\rc,
+    \qquad
+    \operatorname{Rop}_{\rm rad}(3_1)=32.743274,
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_stretch_reference}
+\end{equation}
+with material-volume conservation.  Setting the uniform core radius equal to
+the Planck length gives
+\begin{align}
+    \lambda_P
+    &=
+    \left(\frac{\rc}{\ell_P}\right)^2
+    \simeq
+    7.60\times10^{39},
+    \\
+    L_P
+    &=
+    \lambda_PL_0
+    \simeq
+    3.51\times10^{26}\ \mathrm m,
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_uniform_core_length}
+\end{align}
+using the canonical $\rc$ and the CODATA Planck length
+\cite{Mohr2025CODATA}.  If the canonical circulation is also held fixed and
+$v_\theta(a)\simeq\Gamma_0/(2\pi a)$ is extrapolated to $a=\ell_P$, then
+\begin{equation}
+    v_\theta(\ell_P)
+    \simeq
+    9.54\times10^{25}\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}
+    \simeq
+    3.18\times10^{17}c.
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_uniform_core_speed_obstruction}
+\end{equation}
+Consequently the assumptions
+\begin{equation}
+    a_{\min}=\ell_P,
+    \qquad
+    V_{\rm core}=\mathrm{const.},
+    \qquad
+    \Gamma=\Gamma_0,
+    \qquad
+    v_\theta\simeq\frac{\Gamma}{2\pi a}<c
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_incompatible_assumptions}
+\end{equation}
+cannot all hold simultaneously.  A Planck-scale quantity is therefore retained
+only as a possible local defect, phase-slip, reconnection, or resolution scale;
+it is not identified with the uniform radius of the complete stretched
+electron core.
+
+\paragraph{Promotion gates and falsifiers.}
+The compact-to-slender hypothesis is promoted only if one model and one
+numerical regularization simultaneously provide:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item a continuous topology-preserving path from the certified compact
+    state to a state satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_stretched_state_slenderness};
+    \item a conserved-quantity ledger for volume, circulation, helicity,
+    energy, momentum, and angular momentum;
+    \item a converged $\Delta E_{\rm carrier}(\lambda)$ and a specified excitation
+    channel satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_stretch_energy_gate};
+    \item a finite-core evolution showing that the stretched state is a
+    relative equilibrium, periodic relative orbit, or metastable state;
+    \item no use of $\ell_P$ as a uniform core radius unless the obstruction in
+    Eqs.~\eqref{eq:rt_planck_uniform_core_length}--\eqref{eq:rt_planck_incompatible_assumptions}
+    is removed by an explicit constitutive change.
+\end{enumerate}
+Failure of any item blocks promotion beyond \texttt{[HYPOTHESIS]}.
+
+\subsection{Kirchhoff--Cosserat stationary-shape diagnostic}
+\label{subsec:rt_kirchhoff_cosserat_guard}
+
+\textbf{[BRIDGE / DIAGNOSTIC ONLY].}
+Kirchhoff--Cosserat theory and vortex-filament theory share centreline
+geometry, curvature, torsion, and selected stationary shapes, but they do not
+share the same dynamical phase space.  A schematic inertial rod equation and
+the LIA vortex equation are respectively
+\begin{equation}
+    \rho_{\rm rod}A\,\partial_t^2\mathbf r
+    =
+    -B\,\partial_s^4\mathbf r+\cdots,
+    \qquad
+    \partial_t\mathbf X
+    =
+    \beta\kappa\mathbf b.
+    \label{eq:rt_rod_vortex_time_order}
+\end{equation}
+The former is second order in time and requires position and momentum data;
+the latter is a first-order noncanonical Hamiltonian evolution.  Both can
+produce a $k^2$ frequency scaling after linearization, so the non-equivalence
+must be stated in terms of phase-space and symplectic structure rather than a
+frequency exponent alone.
+
+Fukumoto's result identifies permanent translating vortex-jet-filament shapes
+with Kirchhoff-rod equilibria under localized-induction and axial-core-flow
+assumptions \cite{Fukumoto2007}.  It does not justify importing elastic rod
+normal modes, constitutive moduli, or second-order rod dynamics into SST.
+Accordingly,
+\begin{equation}
+    E_{\rm rod}^{\rm diag}
+    =
+    T L
+    +\frac{B}{2}\int\kappa^2\,ds
+    +\frac{C}{2}\int\Omega_3^2\,ds
+    \label{eq:rt_rod_energy_diagnostic}
+\end{equation}
+may be evaluated on a certified SST centreline only as a comparative diagnostic.
+It is not a derived SST Hamiltonian.  In particular,
+\begin{equation}
+    E_{\rm rod}^{\rm diag}[\mathbf X_{\rm ideal}]
+    \ \text{finite}
+    \quad\not\Rightarrow\quad
+    \left.
+    \frac{\delta E_{\rm rod}^{\rm diag}}{\delta\mathbf X}
+    \right|_{\mathbf X_{\rm ideal}}
+    =0.
+    \label{eq:rt_ideal_not_rod_equilibrium}
+\end{equation}
+A nonzero bending weight generally changes a perfectly flexible ideal-knot
+minimizer; physical elastic trefoils provide a classical comparison
+\cite{JohannsEtAl2021}.  The finite-core Euler/Biot--Savart evolution remains
+the dynamical reference for the SST carrier.
+
+\paragraph{Child-level analogy.}
+A tightly packed soft rope and a bent metal wire can be drawn with the same
+centreline.  That does not make them obey the same motion law when released.
+
